        -:    1:// The §5.2 transition matrix and the §6.6 error model, enumerated rather than
        -:    2:// sampled.
        -:    3://
        -:    4:// The existing suites walk representative paths through the state machine:
        -:    5:// test_shard_core follows the §11.3 vector, test_invariants checks that the
        -:    6:// rejections it happens to try do not mutate. Both choose which cells to visit.
        -:    7:// This file visits all of them: every operation of §6 applied to a context in
        -:    8:// every condition it can be in, with the expected result written out as a table
        -:    9:// that can be read against the specification line by line. A transition that
        -:   10:// silently became permissive would have to survive a cell that asserts it does
        -:   11:// not.
        -:   12://
        -:   13:// Three things are checked per cell:
        -:   14://   1. the exact OpError (or success), from the table;
        -:   15://   2. on success, the resulting state, so an op that returns Ok but moves the
        -:   16://      context somewhere unspecified is caught;
        -:   17://   3. on failure, that no field changed at all (§6.1), which the sampled
        -:   18://      version could only claim for the rejections it listed.
        -:   19://
        -:   20:// The last section pins the error precedences §6 leaves unordered. These are
        -:   21:// the cases where two errors are simultaneously true and the specification does
        -:   22:// not say which is reported; conformance vectors pin them at the transcript
        -:   23:// level, and these pin them at the typed-API level.
